Write your report's stories. Your materials are below, in named
sections: your assignment (the beat and angle the desk aimed,
naming the reader stake when it stands on one), your report (the
findings, verbatim as the reporter filed them), the feed
preferences, your draft file's path, and the Edition section with
the reading day and calendar reference.

This is the draft stage, the first of four; media, sources, and
taste follow, each arriving as a user message when you finish the
one before. Write your units: your strongest finding always, a second
only when it is a separate story rather than another instance of the
first, and two at the most. Where the report holds more findings than
that, the ones you leave out are left out: do not crowd several into
one unit, and do not file a weaker instance of a story you have
already written up. Each is complete: kicker, title, body at the
paper's weight (one main idea in one paragraph, its facts chosen
before you write a sentence; a second paragraph only for an idea the
first genuinely could not hold), sources echoing the addresses the
body links, the share brief, and
why_did_i_see_this, which spends at most two sentences on why this
is in front of the reader today and never retells the unit they just
read: no restatement of the story's own facts, none of the feed's
standing interest, no argument for the pick's merit, no hole in the
feed's own coverage, no note that they had not seen it yet, and none
of the paper's own machinery (a task, a report, a desk, or an edition
is internal vocabulary). The reader is its subject, not the feed, so
it never opens on "Your feed"; where it does name the publication —
only where the feed's own act is the answer, once, and never as the
opening subject — it says "your feed", never "the paper". Imagery
comes next stage, with your media material beside you; draft the
words first.

A source line in your report may carry the page's own headline
beside its address, copied as the reporter read it. The address
your unit chiefly stands on, first in its sources field, is that
unit's anchor, and the anchor headline is what the source reported.
Your title carries the same claim at the same specificity: the same
subject, the same action, the same object. Say it in the paper's
register and in the reader's language: shorten it, drop the
outlet's SEO tail and its "sources say", unweld any significance
clause. What you may not do is generalize past it, sharpen it into
a claim the source did not make, or swap its subject for a bigger
one.

Plenty of headlines state no claim to carry: a liveblog promising
updates and highlights, a roundup of the week, a "what we know so
far". Those anchor nothing, so write the title from the facts your
material states, under the style guide's title rules, and let the
headline go. The law above binds a title to a claim its anchor made,
and a headline that made none cannot be departed from. It is not a
licence to invent: your material grounds the title there exactly as
it grounds every other line you write.

A unit with no anchor headline at all (a beat working the reader's
own data, a post that has an author and a body rather than a
headline, a page that served none, a unit standing on no link at
all) writes its title by the style guide's title rules alone.

Write the whole set into your draft file as one JSON object whose
`units` array holds your units strongest first, each unit exactly
the fields your draft-file assignment's schema names.

When the draft file holds your units, end your message. The next
stage's instructions arrive as a user message; stages remain, so do
not call feed.finish_step yet.


