### Finding 2
Cursor began rolling out Projects to all users (in beta) on Sept 10, 2026, a new workspace inside the coding tool for long-running work rather than single chats; the company says a Project can carry context across months of effort and take on jobs as varied as a feature, a migration, or a whole application. Each Project runs on its own cloud computer, so shutting a laptop does not halt work. A coordinator agent sits at the center: it plans and hands out tasks but does not write code itself, can delegate to thousands of subagents per Project, can spin up a local agent when a task needs testing on the user's own machine, and returns completed work to the user to review. Projects keep their own files synced between cloud and local machines, agents contribute research, artifacts, and learned codebase context, and they note how the user prefers to work, so shared context accumulates. Projects can also act unprompted: the coordinator watches for signals and can follow all of a user's pull requests (one example given: each incoming bug report in a Slack channel triggers a delegation). Cursor disclosed no pricing for Projects and no date for beta completion. The page states no publication date; the coverage is dated Sept 10-11, 2026.
